  </div>

  <AuthorSignal authorId="li-wei" />

    <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-2 gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-200 shadow-sm">
      <div class="bg-white p-5"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-stone-400 uppercase mb-2">Opening Hours</p><p class="text-lg md:text-xl font-semibold leading-tight text-stone-900">8:30 AM – 6:30 PM</p><p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5">Mar-Nov. 8:30-6:00 PM Dec-Feb</p></div>
<div class="bg-white p-5"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-stone-400 uppercase mb-2">Ticket Price</p><p class="text-lg md:text-xl font-semibold leading-tight text-stone-900">$10</p><p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5">March-Nov. $8 Dec-Feb</p></div>
<div class="bg-white p-5"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-stone-400 uppercase mb-2">Time Needed</p><p class="text-lg md:text-xl font-semibold leading-tight text-stone-900">2–3 hours</p><p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5">Museum + burial mound + gardens</p></div>
<div class="bg-white p-5"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-stone-400 uppercase mb-2">Best Time</p><p class="text-lg md:text-xl font-semibold leading-tight text-stone-900">Weekday mornings</p><p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5">Often completely empty</p></div>
<div class="bg-white p-5"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-stone-400 uppercase mb-2">Unique Feature</p><p class="text-lg md:text-xl font-semibold leading-tight text-stone-900">Glass floors</p><p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5">Walk above the burial pits</p></div>
<div class="bg-white p-5"><p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-stone-400 uppercase mb-2">Location</p><p class="text-lg md:text-xl font-semibold leading-tight text-stone-900">Weiyang District</p><p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-1.5">40 min north of downtown</p></div>

    </div>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Why Han Yangling Is the Mausoleum Locals Prefer</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Han Yangling (汉阳陵) is the joint tomb of <strong class="text-stone-800">Emperor Jing of Han</strong> (188-141 BC) and his empress. Built around the same time as the Terracotta Warriors but belonging to a different dynasty, it is often called the <strong class="text-stone-800">"other terracotta army"</strong> — but with a fraction of the visitors.</p>
<p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Unlike the Terracotta Warriors (life-sized soldiers), Han Yangling’s figures are <strong class="text-stone-800">miniature</strong> — about 60 cm tall. They were originally dressed in silk clothing with movable wooden arms. The clothing has long since decayed, leaving naked clay bodies in organized rows. There are also miniature animals, chariots, and everyday objects buried for the emperor’s afterlife.</p>
<p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The museum is built <strong class="text-stone-800">directly over the excavation pits</strong> with glass floors, so you walk above the figurines and see them from directly overhead. The lighting is dim and atmospheric, creating a completely different experience from the bright hangar of the Terracotta Warriors.</p>
<p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">This site gets fewer visitors in a year than the Terracotta Warriors get in a single morning. On a weekday, you may be the only person in the museum. If you want a quiet, contemplative experience of ancient Chinese burial culture without jostling for viewing spots, this is it.</p>

    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Why Han Yangling Is (Arguably) Better Than the Terracotta Warriors</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Han Yangling gets called "the quieter Terracotta Warriors," but that undersells it. For many visitors, it is the <strong class="text-stone-800">more atmospheric experience</strong>. While the Terracotta Warriors are awe-inspiring in scale, Han Yangling offers something the Warriors do not: intimacy.</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">The museum is built directly over the excavation site with <strong class="text-stone-800">glass floors</strong>. You literally walk above the burial pits, looking down at thousands of miniature terracotta figurines — soldiers, servants, animals — exactly where they were placed 2,100 years ago. The figures are smaller (about 60 cm vs life-size) but more detailed — each face is individually sculpted. The burial pits also contain the tomb of Emperor Jing and Empress Wang, making this one of the few Han Dynasty imperial tombs open to the public.</p>
    </section>

<section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Insider Tips</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4"><div class="flex gap-3"><span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-terracotta-100 text-terracotta-700 text-xs font-bold mt-0.5">1</span><p class="text-stone-600"><strong>Weekday mornings are empty.</strong> Han Yangling gets a tiny fraction of the Warriors’ crowds. On a Tuesday at 9 AM, expect to be one of only a handful of visitors.</p></div>
<div class="flex gap-3"><span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-terracotta-100 text-terracotta-700 text-xs font-bold mt-0.5">2</span><p class="text-stone-600"><strong>Combine with downtown attractions.</strong> Visit in the morning (40 min from city center), then return for the City Wall or Muslim Quarter in the afternoon. It pairs well with a relaxed day.</p></div>
<div class="flex gap-3"><span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-terracotta-100 text-terracotta-700 text-xs font-bold mt-0.5">3</span><p class="text-stone-600"><strong>Don’t skip the exhibition hall.</strong> The glass-floored pits are the highlight, but the exhibition hall has fascinating displays about Han Dynasty daily life, including miniature granaries, livestock pens, and pottery kitchens.</p></div>
<div class="flex gap-3"><span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-terracotta-100 text-terracotta-700 text-xs font-bold mt-0.5">4</span><p class="text-stone-600"><strong>The burial mound.</strong> Walk 10 minutes from the museum to the emperor’s actual burial mound — a large grassy hill. You cannot enter it (unexcavated), but standing on top gives a sense of the mausoleum’s scale.</p></div>
</div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900">Nearby</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4"><div class="flex gap-3"><span class="mt-1 flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-sm bg-terracotta-100 text-xs font-bold text-terracotta-700">40 min</span><div><strong class="font-medium text-stone-800">Terracotta Warriors</strong><p class="text-sm text-stone-500">The big one. 40 min east. Do Yangling for a quiet morning, Warriors another day.</p></div></div>
<div class="flex gap-3"><span class="mt-1 flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-sm bg-terracotta-100 text-xs font-bold text-terracotta-700">20 min</span><div><strong class="font-medium text-stone-800">Daming Palace</strong><p class="text-sm text-stone-500">Tang Dynasty palace ruins. Sprawling park with reconstructed gates.</p></div></div>
<div class="flex gap-3"><span class="mt-1 flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-sm bg-terracotta-100 text-xs font-bold text-terracotta-700">30 min</span><div><strong class="font-medium text-stone-800">City Wall North Gate</strong><p class="text-sm text-stone-500">A quieter gate entry point. Good for a partial wall walk.</p></div></div>
</div>
    </section>

        <!-- Related attractions -->
